 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Rendering is $O(R)$ per pixel where $R$ is the number of sampled points along the ray. Training involves matrix multiplications of size $N \times P$ and $N \times C$, where $N$ is the number of query points.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(P \times 7 + P \times C + N)$ for storing primitive parameters, selection matrix, and occupancy fields. With $P=4096$ and $C=256$, this is manageable.
-*   **Measured Performance:** Tested on 4 NVIDIA Tesla V100 GPUs with a batch size of 4 object instances [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Explicit representation allows for structured abstraction of complex topologies (holes, thin structures) [S1]. Differentiable operations allow end-to-end training with image-space losses only [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires careful tuning of the multi-phase test-time adaptation for novel shapes [S1]. Redundant primitives may need to be pruned via dropout [S1].
-*   **When to use:** When explicit, structured 3D abstraction is required from sparse views, and differentiable rendering is needed for optimization [S1].
-*   **When not to use:** When global implicit fields are sufficient or when view-dependent effects are the primary requirement (consider Gaussian Splatting) [S1].
+*   **Complexity:** The method scales with the number of primitives $P$ and convexes $C$. The distance matrix computation is $O(N \cdot P)$, and the convex occupancy is $O(N \cdot C)$. Rendering is $O(R)$ per pixel, where $R$ is the number of samples per ray [S1].
+*   **Performance:** Trained on 4 NVIDIA Tesla V100 GPUs with a batch size of 4 object instances [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Explicit representation allows for compact storage and manipulation of geometry. The differentiable CSG operations enable learning from image-space losses without 3D ground truth. Test-time adaptation allows refinement on novel shapes [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires careful tuning of the multi-phase training. Primitive dropout is computationally intensive if not optimized. Struggles with very complex topologies without silhouette-aware sampling [S1].
+*   **When to use:** Sparse-view 3D reconstruction where explicit geometry is desired, and 3D supervision is unavailable.
+*   **When not to use:** Dense-view scenarios where implicit methods might be faster; real-time applications requiring extremely low latency without pre-computation.
 
 ## Background
-Differentiable Primitive Assembly was introduced in DPA-Net [S1]. It builds on state-of-the-art 3D primitive representation CAPRI-Net [S1]. The method was developed to address the limitations of NeRF variants in abstracting complex 3D structures, such as holes and thin parts, by using a structured assembly of quadric primitives [S1].
+Differentiable Primitive Assembly was introduced in DPA-Net [S1]. It builds on the CAPRI-Net [55] and other works [6, 55] that use quadric primitives. The method was designed to address the limitations of implicit neural representations in capturing sharp features and complex topologies from sparse views, by leveraging explicit primitive assemblies with differentiable set operations [S1].
 
